\description{
Shrinks a vector of sample expected returns toward a lower-variance target,
addressing the well-known sensitivity of mean-variance optimisation (MVO) to
noisy plug-in sample means. Three methods are supported:
\itemize{
\item \strong{\code{"grand_mean"}} — shrinks each asset's expected return toward the
cross-sectional grand mean \code{mean(mu)}. Requires an explicit \code{intensity} δ
or, when \code{n_obs} is supplied, derives a James-Stein-inspired data-driven δ
without needing the covariance matrix. Default δ is 0.5 when neither
\code{intensity} nor \code{n_obs} is supplied.
\item \strong{\code{"james_stein"}} — Bayes-Stein shrinkage (Jorion 1986) toward the
minimum-variance grand mean μ₀ = (1ᵀΣ⁻¹μ) / (1ᵀΣ⁻¹1). The shrinkage
intensity φ is estimated from the data via
φ = (p+2) / ((p+2) + T·(μ−μ₀·1)ᵀΣ⁻¹(μ−μ₀·1)), where p is the number
of assets and T = \code{n_obs}. Requires \code{sigma} and \code{n_obs}.
\item \strong{\code{"equilibrium"}} — shrinks toward the CAPM reverse-optimisation
implied return Π = λ·Σ·w\_mkt, where λ is the market risk-aversion
coefficient and w\_mkt are the market-cap weights. This is the
Black-Litterman prior; the later BL PR will extend this standalone
shrink toward a posterior. Requires \code{sigma}, \code{w_mkt}, and
\code{risk_aversion}. Uses \code{intensity} (default 0.5) for the blend.
}

\strong{Look-ahead bias note:} all intensity parameters (δ, φ) must be estimated
on in-window/training data only. Passing a covariance matrix or \code{n_obs}
estimated on a hold-out period introduces look-ahead bias into the
